# Hide NTFS filesystems on fixed internal disks from udisks, so GNOME stops
# showing a dual-boot Windows partition as a mountable volume in the dock,
# Files sidebar, and desktop. Windows' own recovery partition is already
# hidden upstream by its partition type GUID; the main install uses the
# generic "Basic data partition" type, which upstream cannot distinguish from
# a partition someone does want to mount.
#
# Removable media is deliberately excluded twice over. ATTRS{removable} alone
# is not enough: a USB enclosure commonly reports removable=0 for the drive
# inside it, so an external NTFS disk would match. Internal NVMe and SATA
# devices have no ID_BUS at all, while anything behind USB is tagged
# ID_BUS=usb, and that is what separates them.
#
# Hiding a volume does not unmount it or make it unreachable -- an fstab entry
# or an explicit `udisksctl mount` still works. It only removes the one-click
# affordance for a partition where a stray click can mount a live Windows
# system.
#
# Revert: delete this rule, `udevadm control --reload`, and re-trigger the
# affected partitions (or reboot).
SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_FS_TYPE}=="ntfs", ENV{ID_BUS}!="usb", ATTRS{removable}=="0", ENV{UDISKS_IGNORE}="1"
